  abstract={This paper describes how US Navy Mission Engineering connected to model-based system of systems engineering methods can be applied to modern day naval ship platform design to properly consider and adapt to operational objectives and evolving threats. It incorporates ship design methods and tools in practice at Virginia Tech and integrates them with the ME to SE strategy of the US Navy and Department of War. The paper defines Mission Engineering principles and the current design approach taught at Virginia Tech and provides evidence that demonstrates the importance of maturing Mission Engineering artifacts with a model-based systems engineering development approach and further advancing that into practical ship design methods and tools to ensure achievement of capability in future Naval ship designs. Virginia Tech is collaborating with various offices at NAVSEA to ensure that the approaches and tools that are being proposed support US Navy objectives for the fleet of the future. The intent of this paper is to examine ship design methods, surface and subsurface, and explore how the US Navy could benefit from these methods. The paper closes with recommendations on next step development that merges the needs of the Navy with the most advanced practices that can actually be employed by the ship designers in the Navy and its industrial base.},
